---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ContactBand from '../../components/layout/ContactBand.astro';
import CompoundGrowthInteractionSuite from '../../components/compound-growth/CompoundGrowthInteractionSuite.astro';
import { projects } from '../../data/projects';

const project = projects.find((item) => item.slug === 'compound-growth');
if (!project) throw new Error('Compound Growth project record is missing.');
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Compound Growth & Retirement Investing"
  description="Can identical financial projections produce different judgments depending on how they are shown?"
  canonicalPath={project.route}
>
  <article class="cg-case-study" data-compound-growth-page>
    <CompoundGrowthInteractionSuite>
      <header slot="before-fixed" class="cg-page-hero">
        <p class="cg-page-hero__credential">MSc · Marketing Research & Behavioural Science · University of Ottawa</p>
        <h1>Compound Growth & Retirement Investing</h1>
        <p class="cg-page-hero__lede">Across five randomized experiments, I tested whether identical retirement-growth projections produce different judgments when shown as a numerical table or a line graph.</p>

        <dl class="cg-page-meta" aria-label="Research program at a glance">
          <div><dt>Program</dt><dd>5 randomized experiments</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Final analytic N</dt><dd>1,248 participants</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Population</dt><dd>Young adults who had not begun investing for retirement</dd></div>
        </dl>

        <div class="cg-page-hero__grid">
          <div>
            <p class="cg-page-label">Research question</p>
            <h2>Can identical compound-growth retirement projections produce different judgments when shown as a line graph rather than a numerical table?</h2>
          </div>
          <div class="cg-page-hero__summary">
            <p><strong>My role:</strong> Under faculty supervision, I designed and fielded the experiments, developed the stimuli and measures, analyzed the data, used each study to decide what to test next, and translated the findings into the final manuscript.</p>
            <p><strong>Main finding:</strong> Line graphs made compound-growth acceleration more apparent than tables.</p>
            <p>The results were largely statistically consistent with this pathway: Line graph presentation → greater perceived compound growth acceleration → stronger confidence in investment growth → retirement feels more achievable → higher planned contributions</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </header>

      <section slot="after-fixed" class="cg-narrative cg-narrative--representation" aria-labelledby="representation-title" data-representation-section>
        <div class="cg-narrative__heading">
          <p class="cg-kicker">Representation</p>
          <h2 id="representation-title">Why a table and graph may feel different</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="cg-narrative-grid">
          <div>
            <h3>Table</h3>
            <p>To notice acceleration, the viewer has to compare values across different intervals and recognize that the gains are getting larger.</p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>Line graph</h3>
            <p>The same values form a curve that gets steeper over time, making the accelerating pattern more visible.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="cg-callout">The research question was not whether graphs are universally “better.” It was whether representation changes what people perceive in the same financial projection.</p>
      </section>

      <div slot="before-explorer"></div>

      <div slot="after-explorer" class="cg-page-flow cg-page-flow--after-explorer">
        <section class="cg-narrative" aria-labelledby="limitations-title" data-limitations>
          <div class="cg-narrative__heading">
            <p class="cg-kicker">Limitations</p>
            <h2 id="limitations-title">What the research does not show</h2>
          </div>
          <div class="cg-limitations">
            <p><strong>Planned rather than actual contributions</strong> — The studies measured hypothetical monthly contribution selections, not real deposits.</p>
            <p><strong>A specific population</strong> — Participants were young adults who had not begun investing for retirement.</p>
            <p><strong>A measured pathway</strong> — The intermediate variables were measured rather than independently manipulated, so the statistical models do not prove that each step caused the next.</p>
            <p><strong>No universal graph rule</strong> — Experiment 5 left the exact visual mechanism partly unresolved, and tables can still be better for precise value lookup.</p>
          </div>

          <details class="cg-details">
            <summary>Methods and evidence notes</summary>
            <div class="cg-details__body cg-details__body--columns">
              <div>
                <h3>Study progression</h3>
                <p>Final analytic samples were 295, 141, 97, 182, and 533 across Experiments 1–5. Conditions were randomized within each experiment. Experiments 1–4 compared tables with line graphs; Experiment 5 added proportional area growth cards.</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <h3>Analysis</h3>
                <p>Primary models used age-adjusted regression. Later experiments used bootstrap mediation models to examine statistical indirect pathways. Processing fluency was not consistently higher for line graphs.</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <h3>Projection scope</h3>
                <p>The experiments used simplified deterministic retirement projections to isolate representation. Real-world projections can include return uncertainty, fees, taxes, inflation, changing contributions, and financial constraints.</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <h3>Experiment 4 exclusions</h3>
                <p>Experiment 4 recruited 198 participants aged 24–30. Fourteen were excluded because their growth estimates indicated apparent misunderstanding of the response scale, one was outside the target age range, and one had an undefined Estimated Growth Ratio, leaving a final analytic sample of 182.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </details>
        </section>

        <footer class="cg-source-footer">
          <p class="cg-page-label">Research source</p>
          <p><strong>How Line Graphs of Compound Growth Shape Retirement Investing Judgments</strong></p>
          <p>MSc · Marketing Research & Behavioural Science · University of Ottawa · 2026</p>
        </footer>
      </div>
    </CompoundGrowthInteractionSuite>
  </article>
  <ContactBand />
</BaseLayout>